"<div><div class=\" response-message\"></div></div><div><div class=\" response-message\"><h2>Game Structure Analysis</h2>\n<p>✅ Analysis complete</p>\n<p>This analysis examines a <strong>Repeated Cournot Duopoly</strong>, a foundational model in industrial organization and game theory that explores how firms compete on quantity over time.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>1. Identify the Game Structure</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Game Type:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Non-cooperative:</strong> Each firm makes decisions independently to maximize its own profit, even if their actions affect one another.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-zero-sum:</strong> The total profit in the market is not fixed; both firms can be profitable, or both can suffer losses (e.g., through overproduction).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Temporal Nature:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Repeated Game:</strong> This is not a one-shot interaction. The stage game (Cournot competition) is played multiple times (either infinitely or with an uncertain end date). This allows for the evolution of &quot;supergame&quot; strategies.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Information Structure:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Imperfect Information (within the stage):</strong> In each period, firms choose their quantities simultaneously without knowing the other's choice for that specific period.</li>\n<li><strong>Perfect Information (historical):</strong> Players have &quot;perfect recall,&quot; meaning they can observe the past quantity choices of their competitor before making a move in the next period.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Asymmetries:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Standard models often assume <strong>symmetry</strong> (identical marginal costs and discount factors), but asymmetries can exist if one firm has a lower cost structure or a different valuation of future profits (discount factor $\\delta$).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>2. Define Strategy Spaces</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Available Strategies:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>In the <strong>stage game</strong>, the strategy is the choice of quantity $q_i$.</li>\n<li>In the <strong>repeated game</strong>, a strategy is a complete contingent plan that specifies the quantity to produce in period $t$ based on the entire history of play from periods $1$ to $t-1$.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Discrete vs. Continuous:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Strategies are typically <strong>continuous</strong>. Firms can choose any quantity $q_i \\in [0, Q_{max}]$, where $Q_{max}$ is the quantity at which the market price hits zero.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Constraints:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Non-negativity:</strong> $q_i \\geq 0$.</li>\n<li><strong>Capacity:</strong> Firms may be limited by physical production capacity ($q_i \\leq K_i$).</li>\n<li><strong>Interdependence:</strong> The strategy of one firm is constrained by the &quot;Reaction Function&quot; of the other; the optimal $q_A$ depends on the expected $q_B$.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>3. Characterize Payoffs</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Objectives:</strong> The primary objective is the maximization of the <strong>Present Value (PV)</strong> of the stream of profits over time.\n<ul>\n<li>$\\Pi_i = \\sum_{t=0}^{\\infty} \\delta^t \\pi_{it}$, where $\\delta$ is the discount factor ($0 &lt; \\delta &lt; 1$).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Outcome Dependence:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>The profit for Firm A in any period is $\\pi_A = (P(q_A + q_B) - c_A) \\cdot q_A$.</li>\n<li>Payoffs are highly interdependent: if Firm B increases its quantity, the market price $P$ falls, reducing Firm A's profit even if Firm A's production remains constant.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Transferability:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>Payoffs are generally <strong>non-transferable</strong>. In a non-cooperative duopoly, firms cannot legally engage in side payments (redistributing profits) due to anti-trust regulations.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>4. Identify Key Features</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Commitment and Signaling:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>In a repeated game, current production levels serve as a <strong>signal</strong> of intent. A firm might produce at a &quot;collusive&quot; level (lower quantity) to signal a desire for cooperation.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Coordination (Tacit Collusion):</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>While explicit communication is forbidden, firms can achieve <strong>tacit collusion</strong>. They may use &quot;Trigger Strategies&quot; (like the <strong>Grim Trigger</strong>):\n<ul>\n<li><em>Cooperate:</em> Produce the monopoly quantity (half each) as long as the other does.</li>\n<li><em>Punish:</em> If the opponent cheats, revert to the Nash-Cournot equilibrium quantity forever.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Timing of Moves:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Simultaneous within stages:</strong> Neither firm has a &quot;First-Mover Advantage&quot; (unlike the Stackelberg model).</li>\n<li><strong>Sequential across stages:</strong> The repetition creates a sequential feedback loop where today's move influences tomorrow's environment.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>The Folk Theorem:</strong>\n<ul>\n<li>A key feature of this repeated game is that if the discount factor $\\delta$ is sufficiently high (i.e., firms value future profits enough), any payoff that is better than the Nash-Cournot equilibrium can be sustained as a Subgame Perfect Equilibrium.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Summary Table: Cournot Duopoly Analysis</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr><th align=\"left\">Feature</th><th align=\"left\">Description</th></tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td align=\"left\"><strong>Players</strong></td><td align=\"left\">Firm A and Firm B</td></tr>\n<tr><td align=\"left\"><strong>Primary Choice</strong></td><td align=\"left\">Quantity ($q$)</td></tr>\n<tr><td align=\"left\"><strong>Equilibrium Concept</strong></td><td align=\"left\">Nash Equilibrium (Stage); Subgame Perfect Equilibrium (Repeated)</td></tr>\n<tr><td align=\"left\"><strong>Key Tension</strong></td><td align=\"left\">Individual incentive to increase $q$ vs. Collective incentive to restrict $q$</td></tr>\n<tr><td align=\"left\"><strong>Incentive to Cheat</strong></td><td align=\"left\">High (Short-term gain from &quot;dumping&quot; quantity on the market)</td></tr>\n<tr><td align=\"left\"><strong>Incentive to Cooperate</strong></td><td align=\"left\">High (Long-term stability and higher industry profits)</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</div></div><div><div class=\" completion-message response-message\"></div></div>"